  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSouthwest flow propagating around a 1020 hPa anticyclone\ncentered in the southwestern Atlantic continues to usher sub-\ntropical moisture into the region, leading to extensive cloud\ncover and mere brief breaks of sun. So while it's dry with\npleasant temperatures, we remain in a dreary pattern. This \ntrend is expected to continue through the end of the work week \nwith the return of shower chances. Tomorrow, a weak shortwave \ntrough will drive scattered showers across the Adirondacks where\norographic enhancement will aid in overcoming low-level dry air\nfrom the nearby high. On Friday, a cold front (ana front) will \ntrack through the region, bringing more widespread showers. A \nresulting 0.05\" to 0.5\" of rain is expected with the highest of\nthese amounts likely falling in higher terrain areas to the \nnorth, west, and east of the Hudson River Valley. Saturday will\nbe the first time in several days that consistent sun will be \nseen across the region. \n\nWhile skies remain largely gray over the next couple of days,\ntemperatures will at least remain pleasant and even get quite\nwarm for this time of year. 850 hPa temperatures on the order of\n1 to nearly 2 STDEVs above normal will drive above-normal\nsurface temperatures especially tomorrow and Friday. Tomorrow's\nhighs will subsequently be in the mid 60s to mid 70s before\nFriday's values reach the mid 60s to upper 70s and even pockets\nnear 80 in the lower Mid-Hudson Valley. But, the passage of the\naforementioned cold front will bring temperatures back down\ntowards normal for the weekend before another frontal system\nensures a trend below normal heading into next week. Low\ntemperatures early next week will subsequently largely fall to \nthe 30s, which could pose the threat of frost formation in the\nHudson Valley where the growing season will still be ongoing.",
